text:
@jimmielin1141"national security" is the U.S. "blanket excuse" for everything that they can't compete in. Just ask Huawei, today 5G is the norm, already developed 6G and are finalizing 7G technology.
The real reason was Huawei wouldnt allow the U.S. government back door access to their phones and technology which the U.S. could use to reverse discovery of their technology (to steal it). 
BYD and other Chinese EVs are a threat to the U.S. auto industry.

@qilu2004  well, I am taking about EV, Volkswagen doesn't have sedan but have EV vehicles which is far more reliable than BYD, and think about getting a BYD serviced in Europe, at least in Sweden, it'll be hard to get serviced, we got many Chinese EVs here in Sweden,  problem with them is getting serviced or if something doesn't function well, you gotta wait days to weeks to get fixed. 
Model S is costy compare to other EV vehicles, but you gotta know with 80 grand, you got so much other EV options, at least here in Sweden. Volvo is bringing out EVs with similar price range. You got cheaper options like Kia, hyundai and Nissan.  Tesla's model 3 and y, they cost around 50-65 grand. 
You talking about decent EV,  do you really think BYD is decent EV? They first need to build more service points and then talk about decent or not.

@Victor-vj5ds The 4680 battery you bragged about is still not available for mass production, which is the best proof that Tesla's technology lags behind BYD. In addition, a study by the University of San Diego shows that the energy density of 4680 is much lower than what Tesla claims.
BYD's blade battery has been issued for several years and has long been recognized by the market, when will your 4680 battery be ready for mass production?
Why Tesla is still using CALT and Panasonic batteries?

@jxl594 this all sounds like scripts I've heard, it's the same stuff I've been hearing for years, I could spend a long time picking everything apart you said but that will take a while ranging from BYDs blade battery and it's actual specs to NIOs battery swap station. I've spent hours researching each of these and figuring out their weaknesses just like I did for Tesla for years. You absolutely don't understand what Tesla plans to do in the next 7 years. While your hear talking about electric busses Tesla is planning to make 50,000 Tesla semi trucks a year by 2024 using the largest battery packs ever put into a commercial vehicle, even larger than what you find in the worlds largest battery powered mining trucks. 

Tesla's plant in Mexico is cutting production cost in half, not only that they plan to go 100% in house controllers for their electronics in their next gen mass market vehicles. Tesla is also securing its own lithium refinement, it's not actually that important to make everything yourself unless you know their will be scarcity issues.

But it really feels like you buying into marketing if you think Xpeng is close to Teslas autonomy. They are a theiving company who steal and copy anything they can.
